/**

@page page_exahype_programmers_guide_action_sets How to define what a numerical scheme (or plotter or grid setup) does

\tableofcontents

The @ref page_exahype_programmers_guide_new_solver "introduce a new numerical scheme"
page shows the skeleton: a Solver.NumericalScheme (or Solver.GridConstruction, or
Solver.Plotter) builds a task graph out of peano4.api.taskgraph.Task objects and wires
up their dependencies. That skeleton alone does nothing, though - a bare Task is inert.
This page is about the missing piece: how you actually define *what happens* when one
of these tasks runs.


# Tasks are containers, action sets are the behaviour

A Task corresponds to one grid sweep. What Peano does during that sweep is defined by
the peano4.api.solversteps.ActionSet objects attached to the task via
`task.add_action_set(my_action_set)` (@see @ref page_architecture_home "the architecture
page's discussion of the Hollywood principle" for why control flow works this way: Peano
drives the traversal and calls back into your action sets, not the other way round).

During a grid sweep, Peano's traversal fires a fixed set of events - entered/left a cell,
touched a vertex/face/cell for the first/last time, created a hanging vertex, and so on.
Every ActionSet subclass can react to any subset of these events by overriding
`get_body_of_operation(operation_name)`, where `operation_name` is one of the
`ActionSet.OPERATION_*` string constants (`OPERATION_TOUCH_CELL_FIRST_TIME`,
`OPERATION_BEGIN_TRAVERSAL`, `OPERATION_CREATE_HANGING_VERTEX`, ... - see
`src/peano4/api/solversteps/ActionSet.py` for the full list) and returns a plain C++
code snippet as a Python string. That snippet is spliced verbatim into the generated
observer class at the point where the corresponding event fires.

A minimal action set that does something on every cell looks like this:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
class MyActionSet(peano4.api.solversteps.ActionSet):
    def get_action_set_name(self):
        return __name__.replace(".py", "").replace(".", "_")

    def get_body_of_operation(self, operation_name):
        if operation_name == peano4.api.solversteps.ActionSet.OPERATION_TOUCH_CELL_FIRST_TIME:
            return "// your C++ goes here\n"
        return ""
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

and it is attached to a task, inside your building block's `construct_output()`, with

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
my_task.add_action_set(MyActionSet())
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

A task can have several action sets; they all run, in the order attached, at every
matching event of that task's grid sweep.


# The other hooks besides get_body_of_operation

Besides `get_body_of_operation()`, an ActionSet subclass typically also overrides:

- `get_includes()` - any `#include` statements the snippets above need.
- `get_attributes()` - C++ member variables the generated action-set class should carry
  (e.g. a writer object that is opened in `OPERATION_BEGIN_TRAVERSAL` and used in
  `OPERATION_TOUCH_CELL_FIRST_TIME`).
- `get_constructor_body()` / `get_destructor_body()`.
- `get_action_set_name()` - has to be unique per project; the idiom above
  (`__name__.replace(".py", "").replace(".", "_")`) derives it from the Python module
  path, which is what every existing action set in the code base does.
- `user_should_modify_template()` - return `False` unless you are deliberately shipping
  a class the generated project is meant to overwrite.

Consult the full doc comment on `peano4.api.solversteps.ActionSet` for the lifecycle
guarantees (one instance per grid sweep per tree, copy-constructed per tree for fork/join
parallelism, and so on) before you rely on instance state surviving across events.


# Templating the C++ snippet

Once a snippet grows beyond a line or two of fixed text, hand-building it with string
concatenation gets unreadable fast. Every non-trivial action set in the code base
instead keeps the C++ as a class-level Jinja2 template string and renders it with a
dictionary of substitutions built up in `get_body_of_operation()`, e.g.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
d = {}
d["PREDICATE"] = self.guard
result = jinja2.Template(self.__Template_TouchCellFirstTime).render(**d)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

This keeps the C++ template readable as C++ (rather than as a Python f-string) and keeps
the substitution logic in Python, in line with the design choice documented on the
@ref page_developer_guidelines_home "developer guidelines" page to move away from Jinja
templates that also carry logic - here, the template only ever holds placeholders.


# Reference action sets to study

- `src/peano4/api/actionsets/ConstructRegularGrid.py` - grid setup: refines a regular
  mesh down to a given volume size.
- `src/peano4/toolbox/blockstructured/api/PlotPatchesInPeanoBlockFormat.py` - plotting:
  opens a VTK-style writer in `OPERATION_BEGIN_TRAVERSAL`, dumps one patch per
  `OPERATION_TOUCH_CELL_FIRST_TIME`, and closes the writer in `OPERATION_END_TRAVERSAL`.
  This is the action set @ref page_exahype_programmers_guide_new_solver
  "PlotAsVTKFile" attaches to its plot task.
- `src/exahype2/api/fv/actionsets/` (`InitialCondition.py`, `ReconstructPatchWithHalo.py`,
  `RollOverUpdatedFace.py`, `AdaptivityCriterion.py`, `HandleBoundary.py`) - numerical
  scheme: the guard-predicate pattern (`self.guard`, computed by the surrounding
  Solver.NumericalScheme and passed into the action set's constructor) used to switch
  the generated C++ on or off per cell without baking the condition into the template
  itself is worth studying across all of these.

*/
